WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.002
-Our first guest tonight is an
Academy-Award nominated writer

00:00:03.002 --> 00:00:05.170
and Emmy-nominated actor
you know from his work

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:07.907
in "Eternals," "The Big Sick,"
and "Silicon Valley."

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:10.276
He stars in "Welcome to
Chippendales," which is

00:00:10.276 --> 00:00:12.912
streaming now on Hulu, with
additional episodes premiering

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:14.347
weekly on Tuesdays.

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:16.148
Let's take a look.

00:00:16.148 --> 00:00:18.117
-Is this your place?

00:00:18.117 --> 00:00:20.419
-Oh, this? Yeah.

00:00:20.419 --> 00:00:21.755
I'm the owner.

00:00:21.755 --> 00:00:23.156
-Wow.

00:00:26.493 --> 00:00:28.394
-Are you having a good time?

00:00:28.394 --> 00:00:30.263
-Just not my kind of place.

00:00:30.263 --> 00:00:31.798
No offense.

00:00:35.902 --> 00:00:38.070
-Not my kind of place either.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:41.374
-Please welcome back to the show
our friend Kumail Nanjiani.

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:43.476
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:51.117 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:58.791 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:58.791 --> 00:01:01.895
Welcome back.
-Thank you.

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:03.596
-How are you?
-I'm great.

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:05.031
How are you?
-I'm good.

00:01:05.031 --> 00:01:07.667
How are you doing in the prep,
the run-up to Thanksgiving?

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:09.135
It's a big week
for a lot of people.

00:01:09.135 --> 00:01:10.303
-It is a big week.

00:01:10.303 --> 00:01:12.938
We're actually in the air
on Thanksgiving Day.

00:01:12.938 --> 00:01:15.875
-Oh, you're going to fly?
-I'm going to fly home, yeah,

00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:17.777
because it was either flying
Wednesday, and that's supposed

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:19.946
to be very bad, right?
-Yeah.

00:01:19.946 --> 00:01:21.481
Could be a ghost town
on Thursday, right?

00:01:21.481 --> 00:01:22.949
-I hope so.
-Yeah, that'd be exciting.

00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:24.450
-That's why I'm excited, yeah.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:26.686
But usually we do stuff for,
you know --

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:28.254
We'll do stuff for Thanksgiving.

00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:31.124
My parents get really into
Thanksgiving, you know?

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:33.059
-Really?
-They do.

00:01:33.059 --> 00:01:35.161
And they're like, "You know, we
have to put a lot of masala

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:36.763
on the turkey because we don't
like how it tastes."

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:37.897
[ Laughter ]

00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:40.032
I'm like, "You don't have to
eat turkey."

00:01:40.032 --> 00:01:41.334
They're like,
"It's Thanksgiving."

00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:44.437
[ Laughter ]
I'm like, "You just got here.

00:01:44.437 --> 00:01:47.440
Eat what you like."
But they love it.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:48.908
They got really into it.

00:01:48.908 --> 00:01:52.712
I -- I'm not like a -- Oh,
people are going to get upset.

00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:57.049
I -- I think turkey is not,
like, great-tasting.

00:01:57.049 --> 00:01:58.217
-Yeah, I think you're right.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:58.217 --> 00:02:01.053
-Oh, my God. Really?
-Yeah.

00:02:01.053 --> 00:02:04.957
Yeah, I think, like, the cat's
out of the bag on turkey.

00:02:04.957 --> 00:02:06.726
-Is that okay?
-I think it's fine.

00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:10.363
I mean, if it was good, we'd do
it other times of the year.

00:02:10.363 --> 00:02:12.164
[ Laughter ]
-That's right.

00:02:12.164 --> 00:02:14.567
If there's anything you eat
once a year,

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:16.769
it's probably not that good.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:19.739
-But I remember, you know,
I like tweeted years ago,

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:22.842
I was like, "Let's face it.
Turkey's not that good."

00:02:22.842 --> 00:02:24.811
And people were like,
"Go back to your country."

00:02:24.811 --> 00:02:28.281
[ Laughter ]
It was very intense.

00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:29.649
I was like, "Okay. I won't.

00:02:29.649 --> 00:02:31.617
I'll sit on my opinions about
turkey.

00:02:31.617 --> 00:02:34.120
I'll wait a few years."

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.122
If Mattel wins,
now it's good again.

00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:37.423
-Yeah, it's good again.

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:39.592
It's like everything's different
in Biden's America.

00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:41.060
You're allowed to say what you
want about --

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:42.394
-Finally.

00:02:42.394 --> 00:02:45.097
-You can finally say what you
want about poultry again.

00:02:45.097 --> 00:02:48.500
-Yeah. No, I'm good with
chicken, you know.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.603
I love chicken. It's just --
[ One person clapping ]

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:52.204
Turkey tastes like chicken.

00:02:52.204 --> 00:02:54.474
-Sorry. We have one enthusiastic
chicken fan.

00:02:54.474 --> 00:02:55.541
-Love chicken.
-Yeah.

00:02:55.541 --> 00:02:57.577
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:57.577 --> 00:02:59.479
They misread the room
and thought there would be

00:02:59.479 --> 00:03:01.314
a standing ovation --
-Yeah, for chicken.

00:03:01.314 --> 00:03:04.317
-Chicken! Oh, just me. Just me.
-Okay.

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:06.386
They just have
a big Chick-fil-A bag.

00:03:06.386 --> 00:03:08.487
[ Laughter ]

00:03:08.487 --> 00:03:11.624
I feel like turkey tastes like
chicken having a bad day.

00:03:11.624 --> 00:03:13.560
-Yeah.
-Although, you know, anything

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.027
you're eating probably
had a bad day.

00:03:15.027 --> 00:03:16.596
-Yeah. It's like a chicken --

00:03:16.596 --> 00:03:20.299
[ Laughter ]

00:03:20.299 --> 00:03:21.801
-Very recently.

00:03:21.801 --> 00:03:23.436
It's like, you know,
when you eat steak, you're like,

00:03:23.436 --> 00:03:25.904
"Oh, this is like if a cow was
like, 'Oh, no.'"

00:03:25.904 --> 00:03:29.274
-Yeah. I know, it's true.
Turkeys never -- I think it's

00:03:29.274 --> 00:03:31.343
safe to say turkeys don't
celebrate Thanksgiving.

00:03:31.343 --> 00:03:33.179
-No.
-Or ever talk about what

00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:34.747
they're thankful for
on that day.

00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:37.716
-No. Except this time did you
see Biden --

00:03:37.716 --> 00:03:39.118
-Pardoned a couple, yeah.
-Two!

00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:41.187
-Yeah.
-Called Chocolate and Chip,

00:03:41.187 --> 00:03:43.656
which I think is
very humiliating.

00:03:43.656 --> 00:03:45.591
-Yeah.
-Don't name turkeys

00:03:45.591 --> 00:03:48.227
after better-tasting foods.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:48.227 --> 00:03:49.595
-That's true.

00:03:49.595 --> 00:03:54.266
-Like undeniably chocolate chips
are better than turkey.

00:03:54.266 --> 00:03:56.001
-Do you think that when --
-This is my Hyundai.

00:03:56.001 --> 00:03:58.471
It's called -- Lamborghini is
the name of it.

00:03:58.471 --> 00:04:00.673
[ Laughter ]
-I think, though -- I feel like

00:04:00.673 --> 00:04:03.576
if your turkeys -- I would just
argue that what you should want

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:05.411
to be is named --

00:04:05.411 --> 00:04:07.613
have a nice pairing name
with another turkey.

00:04:07.613 --> 00:04:09.982
That way if there's
a pardoning situation,

00:04:09.982 --> 00:04:12.218
you can't be in the situation --
I'm sure if Biden was like,

00:04:12.218 --> 00:04:13.886
"It's either Chocolate or Chip."
Biden would be like --

00:04:13.886 --> 00:04:15.988
-We got to go them both.
That's right.

00:04:15.988 --> 00:04:18.557
Like, if there's a turkey named
Abbott, I'm like,

00:04:18.557 --> 00:04:21.293
"I'm going to be Costello
because I think we're going to."

00:04:21.293 --> 00:04:22.928
-"That way we might both
get out of here."

00:04:22.928 --> 00:04:25.598
-That was the only twosome
I could think of.

00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:27.166
-No, Abbott and Costello?
-Yeah.

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:29.235
-Yeah, well, you're real Gen Z.

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:30.936
[ Laughter ]

00:04:30.936 --> 00:04:33.539
So, this is a true story about

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:36.275
the founder of Chippendales,
Steve Banerjee.

00:04:36.275 --> 00:04:37.509
Did you know anything about --

00:04:37.509 --> 00:04:40.045
I mean, I knew the basics about
Chippendales.

00:04:40.045 --> 00:04:41.147
-Which is what?

00:04:41.147 --> 00:04:43.082
-Male dancing review,
male strippers.

00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:45.451
-Yes.
-And I mean, I think

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:46.685
that's what most people know.

00:04:46.685 --> 00:04:48.621
I don't know why
it got weird in here.

00:04:48.621 --> 00:04:49.689
-Yeah.

00:04:49.689 --> 00:04:51.724
I mean, that's what
I knew about it.

00:04:51.724 --> 00:04:54.293
But it turns out...
[ Laughter ]

00:04:54.293 --> 00:04:57.429
There's even juicier your stuff
behind the scenes

00:04:57.429 --> 00:04:59.064
than men stripping, which --
-Whoo!

00:04:59.064 --> 00:05:01.600
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:03.602
-There's murder.
You just whooed for murder.

00:05:03.602 --> 00:05:04.937
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:04.937 --> 00:05:06.506
-It's the same chicken person.

00:05:06.506 --> 00:05:08.607
-Yeah, the chicken person's
having a bad night.

00:05:08.607 --> 00:05:11.143
-The chicken person is like,
"I'm going to win him back.

00:05:11.143 --> 00:05:14.613
I'm going to cheer for something
and win him back."

00:05:14.613 --> 00:05:16.849
It's not going to be chicken
or murder.

00:05:16.849 --> 00:05:18.483
Very anti-murder crowd.

00:05:18.483 --> 00:05:21.154
Yeah, so the real guy who
started Chippendales, I didn't

00:05:21.154 --> 00:05:26.459
know, was this Indian immigrant
who was very nerdy and awkward.

00:05:26.459 --> 00:05:30.729
And behind-the-scenes story of
it is wild, dude.

00:05:30.729 --> 00:05:33.566
Like, people got murdered.
He was setting fire.

00:05:33.566 --> 00:05:36.569
So, people stole the idea of
men stripping,

00:05:36.569 --> 00:05:38.103
and he was like,
"That's my idea."

00:05:38.103 --> 00:05:42.542
So he started setting fire
to other male strip clubs.

00:05:42.542 --> 00:05:44.910
-It's a really funny thing to
sort of consider

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:46.078
intellectual property,

00:05:46.078 --> 00:05:48.347
the idea that you invented
male stripping.

00:05:48.347 --> 00:05:50.783
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, exactly. Yeah.

00:05:50.783 --> 00:05:53.852
Like, in front of an audience,
if a guy takes his clothes off,

00:05:53.852 --> 00:05:57.023
"That's my idea. Otherwise,
I'm going to burn you."

00:05:57.023 --> 00:05:59.659
[ Laughter ]

00:05:59.659 --> 00:06:02.862
But it's wild.
He started off, you know --

00:06:02.862 --> 00:06:06.232
He started a backgammon club
first because he was like,

00:06:06.232 --> 00:06:07.500
"There's no --"
-Whoo!

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:09.034
[ Laughter ]

00:06:09.034 --> 00:06:10.603
-Now we're just encouraging
them.

00:06:10.603 --> 00:06:12.904
-Yeah, we are.
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:12.904 --> 00:06:15.207
-Well, if it's the same chicken
and murder person --

00:06:15.207 --> 00:06:19.111
-Yeah. Whoever that is has
the weirdest Yelp history.

00:06:19.111 --> 00:06:20.579
[ Laughter ]

00:06:20.579 --> 00:06:23.081
In New York looking for chicken,
backgammon, and male stripping.

00:06:23.081 --> 00:06:25.784
[ Cheers ]

00:06:25.784 --> 00:06:27.353
-I thought you said yelp as in

00:06:27.353 --> 00:06:29.388
because they were yelping
actually.

00:06:29.388 --> 00:06:31.390
But you meant like --
-I backed myself into

00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:32.491
a classic two meanings.

00:06:32.491 --> 00:06:34.059
[ Laughter ]

00:06:34.059 --> 00:06:36.662
-Classic double entendre.

00:06:36.662 --> 00:06:39.532
He started a backgammon club
because he was like,

00:06:39.532 --> 00:06:41.367
"Backgammon's a great game
that I love,

00:06:41.367 --> 00:06:44.103
and there are
no backgammon clubs in L.A.,

00:06:44.103 --> 00:06:46.272
so there's a whole market
for it, right?"

00:06:46.272 --> 00:06:47.907
And obviously, it didn't work.

00:06:47.907 --> 00:06:50.909
He called it Destiny 2
because he was like --

00:06:50.909 --> 00:06:52.178
-The name of the backgammon
place?

00:06:52.178 --> 00:06:54.480
-The name of the backgammon
place was Destiny 2.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:57.850
There was no Destiny 1, but he
was like -- he was like,

00:06:57.850 --> 00:07:00.186
"It's a sequel so people will
think, oh, the first one was

00:07:00.186 --> 00:07:03.989
so successful,
I had to open Destiny 2."

00:07:03.989 --> 00:07:07.426
There was no Destiny 1, and
obviously, that didn't go well.

00:07:07.426 --> 00:07:11.630
And then he got the idea for men
stripping in front of women,

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:13.199
and then it became a huge hit.

00:07:13.199 --> 00:07:15.434
-And so, the name Chippendales,
as well, which again,

00:07:15.434 --> 00:07:18.203
the first time I heard
Chippendales was the dancers.

00:07:18.203 --> 00:07:20.439
And then I realized it was
a furniture --

00:07:20.439 --> 00:07:22.074
like a famous furniture
designer.

00:07:22.074 --> 00:07:23.709
But he did name it after that,
yes?

00:07:23.709 --> 00:07:25.478
-Yeah, Thomas Chippendale.
-Yeah.

00:07:25.478 --> 00:07:28.781
-Thomas Chippendale was
a renowned 18th-century

00:07:28.781 --> 00:07:32.151
cabinet maker,
and, like, royalty would have

00:07:32.151 --> 00:07:34.653
his cabinets in their house,
and this guy, Steve Banerjee,

00:07:34.653 --> 00:07:37.757
was like -- He wanted to be,
like, classy, you know?

00:07:37.757 --> 00:07:39.458
And he was like men stripping
for women

00:07:39.458 --> 00:07:41.160
isn't inherently classy,

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:42.995
not that it's not,
but it's not classy.

00:07:42.995 --> 00:07:45.231
So he was like, "If I name it
something super classy,

00:07:45.231 --> 00:07:46.765
it'll trick people."
-Yeah.

00:07:46.765 --> 00:07:48.433
-Like, you know how people line
up to go see,

00:07:48.433 --> 00:07:50.670
like, fancy furniture at night?

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:51.770
[ Laughter ]

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:54.540
Like, ooh, bachelorette party.

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:57.276
Let's go see some furniture,
you know?

00:07:57.276 --> 00:07:59.879
We had some good wood
on the show.

00:07:59.879 --> 00:08:01.447
[ Laughter ]

00:08:01.447 --> 00:08:03.716
-That's your yelp.
That's your yelp.

00:08:03.716 --> 00:08:05.384
I have a bunch more questions.

00:08:05.384 --> 00:08:08.020
Let's let them -- Let's let
them, you know, simmer in that.

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:10.489
-Yeah. Reset yourselves.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:10.489 --> 00:08:12.859
-We'll be right back with
more from Kumail Nanjiani.

